  "Chase": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Basic Rules\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>This game is played with a hanafuda deck.\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Matching a card in your hand with one of the table allows you to take that card.\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The object of the game is to form special card combinations to outscore your opponents.\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> There are twelve card suits consisting of the months of the year (January to December).\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> Each suit consists of four cards.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Basic Rules\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>When it's your turn, place one card from your hand on the table.\n\n　　If there is another card of the same suit on the table: <symbol=case_file_right_arrow> You collect the pair.\n　　If there are no other cards of the same type on the table: <symbol=case_file_right_arrow> Your card stays on the table.\n\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>When you form a specific combination with acquired cards, you can select whether to call \"Koi\" and keep going.\n\n　　Call \"Koi\"　 <symbol=case_file_right_arrow> The hand continues and you try to form more combinations.\n　　Don't Call \"Koi\" <symbol=case_file_right_arrow> The hand ends and you get the points for the card combinations you hold.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Junk (1 Point)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Any ten junk cards, with each additional junk card adding another point.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Sake Cup with Chrysanthemum can also be counted as a junk card.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Poetry Ribbons (1 Point)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Any five poetry ribbon cards, with each additional poetry ribbon card adding another point.": "",
  "Koi-koi Combinations": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Variety (1 Point)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Any five 10-point cards, with each additional 10-point card adding another point.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Blue Poetry Ribbons (5 Points)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The Chrysanthemum, Peony, and Maple poetry ribbon cards.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Red Poetry Ribbons (5 Points)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The Pine, Sakura, and Plum poetry ribbon cards.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Boar, Deer, Butterfly (5 Points)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The Boar with Clover, Deer with Maple, and Butterfly with Peony cards.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Moon Viewing (5 Points)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The Moon over Pampas and Sake Cup with Chrysanthemum cards.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Cherry Blossom Viewing (5 Points)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The Curtain with Sakura and Sake Cup with Chrysanthemum cards.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Seasons (4 Points)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Four cards of the same month as the current round.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Three Lights (6 Points)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Any three 20-point cards, except Rain Man.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Rainy Four Lights (7 Points)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Rain Man with three other 20-point cards.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Four Lights (8 Points)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Any four 20-point cards, except Rain Man.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Five Lights (10 Points)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Rain Man with four other 20-point cards.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Four of a Kind (6 Points)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Having four cards of the same month in your hand gives you 6 points and concludes play for that month.\n\n\n\n<symbol=case_file_head>Four Pairs (6 Points)\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Having four pairs with matching months in your hand gives you 6 points and concludes play for those months.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Oicho-kabu Rules\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>This game is played with a hanafuda deck, but only the cards from January to October are used.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Each month has a numeric value equal to where it is on the calendar (e.g. January is 1 and October is 10).\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Players are each dealt two or three cards, which they then total up.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>For totals over 10, only the last digit (ones place) is used. The player closest to 9 wins.\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>There are four players, including a dealer.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The dealer plays against the other players, who place the bets.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The dealer changes when a round ends.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Oicho-kabu Rules\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Four cards are dealt face-up on the table.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Each player selects one card from the four on the table as the one to bet on.\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Next, each player receives an additional card from the deck.\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The non-dealer players may stay with two cards or draw a third card to go into the showdown with a total of three cards.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Players who choose a third card may draw it from the deck at this time.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Oicho-kabu Rules\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The dealer draws once the three other players are staying.\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>After drawing two cards, the dealer may choose to draw a third.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The dealer may also choose which players to challenge with two cards and which to challenge with three.\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Once the winners are decided and the points calculated, the dealer changes and the game proceeds to the next round.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Score Calculation\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>A Four-One and a Nine-One each pay double your bet, while a three of a kind triples it.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>A Ten-Ten-One increases the pay by 20.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>For all other hands, the payout is the same as your bet.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Hands\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Normal Hand: When the dealer and player have the same hands, the dealer wins.\n\n　　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Zero<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> When the last digit of the total is 0.\n　　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>One<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> When the last digit of the total is 1.\n　　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Two<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> When the last digit of the total is 2.\n　　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Three<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> When the last digit of the total is 3.\n　　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Four<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> When the last digit of the total is 4.\n　　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Five<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> When the last digit of the total is 5.\n　　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Six<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> When the last digit of the total is 6.\n　　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Seven<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> When the last digit of the total is 7.\n　　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Eight<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> When the last digit of the total is 8.\n　　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Nine<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> When the last digit of the total is 9.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Hands\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Some cards can form special hands.\n\n　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Four-One<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> The first two cards dealt were 4 and 1.\n　　　　　　　　　　　  Valid only for non-dealers. Pays double the bet.\n\n　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Nine-One<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> The first two cards dealt were 9 and 1.\n　　　　　　　　　　　 Valid only for the dealer. Pays double the bet.\n\n　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Three of a Kind<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> Three cards all have the same value.\n　　　　　　　　　　　 Valid for dealer and players. Pays triple the bet.\n\n　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Ten-Ten-One<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> Two cards have the value of 10 and one is a 1.\n　　　　　　　　　　　 Valid for dealer and players. Pays 20 times the bet.\n\n　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Four-Six<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> The first two cards dealt are 4 and 6.\n　　　　　　　　　　　 Valid for dealer and players. The player can choose to remove themselves from the current round.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Hands\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The strength of normal/special hands are as follows.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The dealer's Ten-Ten-One is the strongest. Zero is weakest.\n\n\n　　　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Strongest<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　Dealer's Ten-Ten-One\n　　　　　　　Player's Ten-Ten-One\n　　　　　　　Dealer's Three of a Kind\n　　　　　　　Player's Three of a Kind\n　　　　　　　Nine-One\n　　　　　　　Four-One\n　　　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Weakest<symbol=case_file_greater_than>　Zero to Nine": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Texas Hold'em Rules\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Texas Hold'em is a poker game in which you build five-card hands from\n　　seven cards (your own two hole cards, plus five community cards).\n\n\n<symbol=case_file_lower_than>How to Win<symbol=case_file_greater_than>\n　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> Have the strongest hand at the showdown.\n　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> All the other players fold.": "",
  "Poker (Texas Hold'em)": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Flow of the Game\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>In the first round, each player is dealt two hole cards. Three community cards are dealt in the second round. The fourth and fifth\n　　community cards are dealt in the third and fourth rounds, respectively.\n\n\n　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Advancing to the Next Round<symbol=case_file_greater_than>\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow>All players have placed bets of the same amount.\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow>Once All Players Have Checked:\n\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>At the end of the fifth round, all players reveal their hands in a showdown. \n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The player with the strongest hand wins.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>How a game of \"Texas Hold'em\" goes\n\n　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Possible Actions in Each Round<symbol=case_file_greater_than>\n\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow>Bet: Bet a fixed amount of chips.\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow>Call: Bet the same amount of chips as the previous player.\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow>Raise: Bet more chips than the previous player.\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow>Check: Don't bet, in which case the next player goes.\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow>Fold: Stop betting and retire from the current hand.\n\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>You may place a bet if none of the other players have bet yet.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>You can check if none of the other players have bet yet, or if all the other players call the big blind. ": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Flow of the Game\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>A big blind and little blind are forced bets at the beginning of a hand that must be made by the first and second players that\n　　get the dealer button after the current dealer. (The dealer button rotates clockwise after each hand.)\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Bet Amounts Each Round\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow>Big Blind: Minimum bet\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow>Little Blind: Half the minimum bet": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>One Pair\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Two cards of the same rank": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Two Pair\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Two cards of one rank + two cards of another rank": "",
  "Poker Hands": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Three of a Kind\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Three cards of the same rank": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Straight\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Five cards of sequential rank\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> (A, 2, 3, 4, 5 is the weakest straight)": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Flush\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Five cards of the same suit": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Full House\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>One pair + three of a kind": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Four of a Kind\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Four cards of one rank": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Straight Flush\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>A straight of the same suit": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Royal Flush\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>A straight comprised of A, K, Q, J, 10, all of the same suit": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>High Card\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>A hand that does not contain any of the previous combinations\n\n\n<symbol=case_file_head>Tie Break Rules\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>1. The player who holds the hand with the highest card wins.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>2. The player who has the highest card wins.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>3. If all card ranks match, it is a draw and the pot is split.": "",
  "Mahjong: Rules": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Mahjong Rules\n\n　<symbol=case_file_lower_than>Basic Rules<symbol=case_file_greater_than>\n\n　　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Four players compete to be the first to complete a 14-tile hand.\n　　<symbol=case_file_subhead>This hand is typically composed of 4 groups of 3-tile combinations and 1 pair.\n　　<symbol=case_file_subhead>The point value of a player's hand is determined by the tiles they have collected.\n　　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Each player takes turns drawing and discarding tiles to construct their hand to reach the win condition.\n　　<symbol=case_file_subhead>In addition to drawing them, players can steal certain tiles that their opponents discard. ": "",
  "　　<symbol=case_file_subhead>As you play through a game, you will draw and discard tiles while trying to form sets of straights and three-of-a-kinds\n　　　to meet the win condition.\n　　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Sometimes you can steal from others players to reach that goal quicker.\n　　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Aim to form 4 sets of 3 (straights or triplets) and 1 pair.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Shuntsu and Kotsu\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Among the Mentsu, there are Shuntsu and Kotsu.\n\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> A Shuntsu is 3 consecutive numbers of the same suit.\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> A Kotsu is a set of 3 identical tiles.": "",
  "\n<symbol=case_file_head>Janto\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Janto is made by 2 of the identical tile, and called a Toitsu.\n\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> Toitsu is a pair of 2 of the same tile.": "",
  "<symbol=case_file_head>Melds\n\n\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Immediately after an opponent discards a tile, you can claim and add it to your hand, creating a \"meld.\"\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>Melds are divided into pon, chi, and kan, explained on the next page.\n　<symbol=case_file_subhead>A concealed hand is when Pon, Chi, or Kan has not been declared.\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> There are hands that can only be formed through a concealed hand.\n　　<symbol=case_file_right_arrow> Also, there are some hands that will have their scoring elements taken down if they have melded.": ""
 },
